        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"dumas-fils-la-dame-aux-camelias-3eef224dc85adf28\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.95,\"analysis\":\"Marguerite’s embedded “je t’aime” presents her love for Armand as a current fact and as the standard for the reciprocal love she requests from him. It is principally a truth-conditional avowal. Although it supports an appeal involving trust and a shared plan, the highlighted clause does not itself undertake a commitment. Nothing indicates reflex-like expressive discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The avowal may also serve strategically to reassure Armand and secure his cooperation, but that contextual function does not give the core clause independent performative or expressive/reflexive force.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Marguerite directly says “je t’aime” within the comparative clause “comme je t’aime” in her request that Armand love her correspondingly.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Marguerite invokes her professed love while asking Armand to trust her undisclosed financial plan and continue loving her. Her later elaboration—repeatedly affirming that she loves him and envisioning a summer together—supports a sincere-seeming avowal, while Armand’s suspicions create contextual uncertainty about the plan rather than about the semantic mode of the highlighted clause. The utterance is relationally consequential and potentially persuasive, but remains an assertion of present love rather than an explicit undertaking or affective verbal reflex.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Non, il faut seulement que tu m’aimes comme je t’aime, et tout réussira.”\",\"supports\":\"The comparative “comme je t’aime” presents Marguerite’s own present love as true and uses it as the measure of the love requested from Armand.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Marguerite explains that her plan would leave her free of debt and allow them to spend the summer together in the country.\",\"supports\":\"The avowal occurs within an appeal for trust and cooperation, explaining its persuasive relational role without making the highlighted clause itself a commitment.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Later Marguerite says, “j’ai la folie de vous aimer” and “rappelez-vous seulement que je vous aime.”\",\"supports\":\"Her repeated explicit avowals reinforce the interpretation of the highlighted words as presenting an enduring emotional state rather than as an isolated reflexive discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is identified as affirmative present-tense “je t’aime” in Chapter XIII of La Dame aux camélias.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms the grammatical and bibliographic identification of the classified occurrence.\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T/P/E/O naturally represents the core utterance as a strong truth-conditional avowal. Its persuasive use, the secret plan, and possible questions about sincerity belong to contextual interpretation and create no residual pressure on the ontology.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
